  ($mig$HealthIT_News$mig$, $mig$Study examines surgical price tags, finds massive markups$mig$, $mig$https://www.techtarget.com/revcyclemanagement/news/366632292/Study-examines-surgical-price-tags-finds-massive-markups$mig$, $mig$The study found that hospitals frequently charge more than 17.5 times the cost for surgical care, but facilities with the highest price markups tend to have worse care quality. The researchers identified 3.0 as the median hospital price markup ratio, which represents the difference between what the hospital billed for a care episode and the actual costs incurred for it. However, about 40% of the hospitals charged more than four times the cost of inpatient hospitalizations, and 10% charged more than eight times. The study suggests that high-markup hospitals are not only linked to lower quality of care but also value of care.$mig$, $mig$ronstik - stock.adobe.com News Study examines surgical price tags, finds massive markups A new study shows significant hospital pricing markups, with the high-cost hospitals for elective surgical care also experiencing more adverse care outcomes. Sha$mig$, '2026-05-21 19:58+00'::timestamptz),
